[Music]  once again this holiday season a  glorious christmas tree with over 7 000  lights grace's historic boston common  the tree itself is anything but local  though 50 years ago the canadian  province of nova scotia sent boston a  soaring evergreen and thanks for the  city's help after their capital of  halifax was devastated by a deadly  explosion  the tradition has continued every year  since  nancy chen shows us what it means for  both communities  [Applause]  about 40 miles outside halifax nova  scotia on the gourley's fifth generation  dairy farm a tree so tall it still holds  the lights strung some 20 christmases  past  having outgrown the family's highest  ladder long ago  we couldn't prune it anymore after that  we had to stop decorating because you  know  betty the girlies matriarch says the  white spruce has been growing right  alongside her family for nearly four  decades now this is rudolph and this is  dancer  but as a tree in waiting it's still a  few feet short from its eventual destiny  more than 600 miles away and across an  international border  chester gourley has been eagerly  anticipating his family's turn it's been  five or eight years since they said they  might consider us  it's something we've been waiting for  and  it's a big honor and a tradition dozens  of nova scotian families have performed  before them the spruce the province  sends each year becomes boston's  official christmas tree a gesture of  appreciation more than a hundred years  after the halifax explosion the largest  man-made explosion until hiroshima  happened here in the harbor  december 6  1917.  new glasgow's town crier james stewart  has accompanied the tree to boston in  past years during world war one and  halifax  was basically a hubbub of ships that  were getting ready to convoy across the  atlantic on the day of the explosion  that included the ss mont blanc on its  way to france when the ss emo crashed  into it in the narrowest part of the  harbor when the collision took place  sparks basically flew  and one of the ships had munitions on  the deck it was in a nutshell overloaded  just 20 minutes later as people gathered  to see what happened the ship erupted  the city was basically  flattened the north end of the city  two thousand people were killed and an  estimated 9 thousand people injured many  by shattering glass nearly 600 people  were treated for eye injuries to make  matters worse rescue efforts were  hampered by a blizzard blanketing the  region the next day stalling travel from  nearby major cities boston was the best  bet because it was a city that was big  enough close enough  and they dispatched the relief trains  based on a single plea for help by  telegraph medical teams from  massachusetts set out that night their  train making its way through the snow to  set up relief camps in the city what did  their arrival mean to the people of  halifax at the time it's  you know it's very much like the cavalry  arriving they brought halifax aid  when halifax needed it  the absolutely the most  and  it's something that's never been  forgotten  in large part because of customs like  this a grateful halifax delivered boston  a christmas tree the next year a gift  repeated in 1971 and every year since  what do you look for in the tree for  boston we look for a tree that's about  45 to 50 feet tall as manager of the  tree for boston program sandra johnston  considers contenders from across nova  scotia keeping an eye on about 15 trees  you have a list going essentially for  many christmases in the future yes we do  and i don't think i could sleep at night  if i didn't have a list of  trees coming up in the future trees are  nominated by the public as well as  landowners volunteering their own you're  sending your best and brightest to a  place that's pretty far away yes but you  know as most nova scotians love and  appreciate uh they remember the  assistance that boston gave us 104 years  ago and you don't have to go too far  when talking to anybody from nova scotia  to find that connection  a bond forged in tragedy now  commemorated each year as the tree makes  the long trip from nova scotia to boston  happy holidays boston enjoy your tree  it's on the way  communities line the truck's route with  sendoff parades as the journey overcomes  winter storms city traffic and even the  pandemic last year as land borders were  closed to non-essential goods the tree  traveled by container ship  a journey repeated this year  [Applause]  the 60-year-old tree from a cape  brighton non-profit cheered as it was  lit on the bus in common with the  premiere of nova scotia attending the  ceremony alongside boston mayor michelle  wu this is a symbol of what we can do  when we step up to see how we can be  there for each other so i'm incredibly  grateful to nova scotia for continuing  this important tradition a tradition the  gourley family is proud to partake in  one day the tree towering over their  front yard just a small token of  appreciation for the help from a far  away city so many years ago what does it  mean to you to have this tree  potentially go to boston well they  helped us  enormously and this is just a thank you  a community offering a helping hand when  it was most needed as gratitude endures  generations for cbs saturday morning  nancy chen halifax nova scotia

